WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.670
Roll up, roll up, step right this way, ladies
and gentlemen!

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:08.269
On the other side of these canvas doors, you
are about to witness something so monstrous,

00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:14.580
so hideous, so repulsive that it will turn
your stomachs and fill you with amazement.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.020
The creature you'll find in this tent has
been known to elicit all manner of responses

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.740
from well-to-do folks like yourselves.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.950
You may want to laugh at it, you may want
to strike it down, you may even want to throw

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.000
rotten fruit at it, and believe you me, we
are prepared for that.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.070
You will find all manner of rotten tomatoes,
eggs, and other festering foods that are begging

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:39.020
to be pelted, for the low, low price of five
cents per handful.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.840
If you follow me through these doors and gather
around this cage quietly, perhaps you will

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:44.890
see it.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.520
Wait for your eyes to adjust to the dark and
hold your revulsion until you cannot bear

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.020
it anymore.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:56.590
Only then we get to look at the papery gray
skin sagging loose over a swollen gut.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:01.800
Marvel at its tree trunk legs, barely strong
enough to support its own weight, its deformed

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.510
trunk that hangs limp off the front of its
face, and its ridiculous ears that flap nervously

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.200
when it's afraid.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.710
Don't be afraid of the tusks sticking out
from its face or its cries of pain and anguish.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.380
Instead, hold your tongues and wait, for if
you're patient enough, and perhaps with a

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:23.840
slight beating, it may even play us a song
with the valves on its trunk.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.540
Then and only then, you're permitted to pelt
the Elephant Man with whatever you see fit,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.700
as you bask in the glory of Herman Fuller's
Circus of the Disquieting!

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.700
*

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.799
Reese Freeman didn't have much choice but
to join the circus at the age of 14.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:44.649
The cast-out son of a freed slave in New Orleans,
he spent much of his early life wandering

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:47.600
the streets, trying not to get in any trouble.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.270
In the late 1800s, very few places were interested
in hiring a young black man, unless, of course,

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.549
they were looking to turn him back to the
life that his father had fought so hard to

00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:58.549
escape.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.760
That's why when Reese was out fishing one
afternoon and saw a big red tent going up

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.590
on the outskirts of town, he couldn't help
but wander over.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.729
Much to his surprise, a group of black men
were the ones raising the marquee.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.379
They didn't look like slaves, either.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:15.550
Reese had never heard of a circus.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.400
The closest he'd ever come to seeing one was
a traveling entertainment cart that would

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.660
sometimes pop up around town with a dancing
bear in a cage or a glass ball and a pack

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.190
of tarot cards.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:30.590
The brightly colored tents that went up one
after another were enough to draw a crowd,

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.290
even the most well-respected in New Orleans
came out for a look.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.620
Standing ankle-deep in the river, Reese peered
over at the man who came out to address the

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.620
throng.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:44.629
Dressed in a long tailcoat with a tall top
hat, the man stood on a box of fruit and waved

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:46.660
a cane dramatically in the air.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:52.040
"Good folks of New Orleans, what you're about
to witness tonight has never before been seen

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.120
this side of the Mississippi.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:00.840
Wonders and amazement galore, spectacle and
intrigue, mystery and mayhem, and just a touch

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.840
of the impossible.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.500
I go by the name of Herman Fuller, the most
talented and notorious circus master in the

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.590
entire United States.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.930
The dazzling array of shows that I've brought
before you is not for the faint of hearts

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.549
or the weak of constitution.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:22.500
Your stomachs will be turned, and your minds
may be altered, but it promises to be a night

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.340
that you will never forget."

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.390
Reese wasn't much interested in any of what
he had just said.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:32.600
This kind of two-penny show was reserved for
the folks who had the money to throw around

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.280
on needless things like smiling and having
a nice evening.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:41.630
What Reese was far more interested in were
the groups of men working behind the scenes,

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.340
pulling various carts, and setting up all
of the stalls.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.170
A lot of them didn't look much older than
him.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.580
A grin spread across his face.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.580
*

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.349
SCP-4409 insisted on being interviewed in
the dark.

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:58.670
With all the lights in the interview chamber
switched off, Dr. Simon Crossley entered the

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.049
room and sat down across from the Elephant
Man.

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:06.829
Even in the dim light, though, he was still
able to make out part of the man's features:

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:12.050
the ivory tusks, the hanging nose, and the
nervously twitching ears.

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:16.320
The man had been picked up in St. Louis, where
local police had found him panhandling in

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.320
the streets.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:21.290
While he certainly seemed harmless enough,
it was important for the Foundation to take

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.730
him in just to be sure.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:28.080
Sitting in the dark interview room, Reese
Freeman was apprehensive at first about telling

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.070
his side of the story.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:35.539
A life of being mocked, spat on, and chased
out of towns had left him with a real distrust

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:36.949
of other people.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:42.979
That was probably made worse by his apprehension
at being put in another cage within the Foundation.

00:04:42.979 --> 00:04:45.960
He told Dr. Crossley that he was born as normal
as could be.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.449
That his current ghastly affliction was something
done to him quite some time ago, but as much

00:04:51.449 --> 00:04:56.560
as it pained him to recollect, it was a tale
that he knew he’d probably have to tell.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.990
Dr. Crossley replied that he was all ears.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.810
It took Dr. Crossley a few seconds to realize
his faux pas.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:10.069
SCP-4409 glared at him before continuing to
explain how he was made the way he was.

00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:11.069
*

00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:14.699
Reese Freeman had managed to land a job with
the circus surprisingly easily, despite having

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:18.289
no previous experience and no references to
back him up.

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:20.310
Herman Fuller took a shine to him.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.940
It was menial work, and not many folks were
up to doing it, but since Reese had never

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.750
had a job before, he didn't know any better.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.790
He started out at the bottom of the ladder,
scraping out feces, washing down old cages,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.630
and carrying heavy crates.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:39.870
The once scrawny 14-year-old boy quickly turned
into a stocky 17-year-old with broad shoulders

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.180
and strong legs.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.820
They would travel from town to town across
most of the United States, stopping off in

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.580
major cities for weeks at a time to bring
in as much money as possible before moving

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.580
on.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.580
Reese didn't save much of that money, but
he didn't mind.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:58.500
The fact that he got two meals a day and something
to do with himself was enough.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.080
In fact, he kind of enjoyed sleeping on top
of the carts as the horses pulled them along

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.070
the dusty American roads.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.669
The one thing that always seemed to affect
him, though, was that he was never allowed

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:11.449
to actually see inside the tents.

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:16.130
He'd see folks come and go through the canvas
doors week after week but was never allowed

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:19.040
so much as a peek inside himself.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.070
The older guys were responsible for throwing
the canvas sheets over the cages, which would

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.400
remain that way as they traveled.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:29.509
Not having seen much of the world at that
point, Reese didn't have much of an idea of

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:31.840
what could be in those cages.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.370
Perhaps some kind of animal from a foreign
country, something from Africa or Asia.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:39.069
They made all kinds of noise.

00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:43.250
Reese would say that most of them sounded
like humans, but then they'd go and make strange

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.710
animal sounds that no human mouth could ever
produce.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.690
One night, his curiosity got the better of
him.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:54.139
Hanging back before the crowds got into the
tent, he sidestepped over to the entrance,

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:58.570
making sure that no one saw him, and took
a peek through the doorway.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.560
It was dark inside, too dark to make out much
of what was happening.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.970
The crowd of people was standing between him
and the cage, all jostling and leaning this

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.690
way and that, trying to make out whatever
it was that was inside.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:16.610
A screeching sound filled the air, making
the whole crowd and Reese outside jump right.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:21.340
Standing on his tiptoes, he tried his best
to see what was moving around in that cage.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:26.229
It seemed to be moving around like a person,
lurching this way and that, but the sounds

00:07:26.229 --> 00:07:29.270
coming from it sounded more like a bird.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:34.610
And sure enough, after a moment, a huge pair
of disproportionately ugly wings stretched

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.270
out from either side of the creature, flapping
and sending a rush of air through the tent

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:45.120
so powerful that it blew the flap wide open
in front of him, just as Herman Fuller turned

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.710
around to see him standing there.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.050
Reese bolted and hid behind one of the carts.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.889
He was going to be in trouble for this, he
was sure of it.

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:58.280
It was one of Fuller's only rules of the circus,
and he'd just gone and broken it.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.740
He was in the hottest water imaginable.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:06.139
But as the nighttime fell and the dregs of
the spectators wandered out of the tent, Fuller

00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:08.479
didn't seem to be in a bad mood at all.

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:12.310
Watching him from a distance, Reese got the
impression that he might not actually be in

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.349
as much trouble as he thought.

00:08:14.349 --> 00:08:18.830
Fuller caught his eye and came over, cane
twirling and top hat sitting jauntily atop

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:19.940
his head.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:24.330
Reese got off on the wrong foot, apologizing
right away for spying into the tent.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.430
The circus master just laughed it off and
slapped him on the back.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.771
‘Reese, my boy, don't worry about it.

00:08:29.771 --> 00:08:33.870
I've got much bigger plans for you, much,
much bigger.’

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.909
That night, as Reese lay in bed, he had a
terrible dream about what he'd seen in the

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:39.100
tent that day.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:43.399
"Circus of the Disquieting" was certainly
an apt title for this place.

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:49.700
The image of that strange shadowy bird person
in the cage haunted his nightmares until suddenly,

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.990
two pairs of strong hands gripped him and
ripped him out of his sleep.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:55.899
He knew better than to fight back.

00:08:55.899 --> 00:09:00.280
This was surely going to be his comeuppance
for breaking Fuller's rules earlier.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.829
They dragged him into the main tent where
there was an oil lamp in the middle, illuminating

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:07.420
what looked like a heavy wooden operating
table.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:12.050
His wrists and ankles were strapped in, and
then two men left him alone.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:16.760
He had half a mind to scream, but with all
the caged-up creatures from the circus around

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.820
him, he doubted anyone would pay him much
mind.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.730
It was almost an hour before Herman Fuller
walked into the tent.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:28.940
By this point, Reese expected him to be walking
in with surgical equipment, gloves, maybe

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:33.270
even a saw, but the man had nothing in his
bare hands.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:36.940
Herman Fuller asked him if he’d ever heard
of Joseph Merrick.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.260
Reese didn't answer.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.450
Of course, he'd heard of the Elephant Man.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:43.860
The whole world had been talking about Joseph
Merrick for months.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:49.079
A man born so hideously deformed that people
had compared him to an elephant, though the

00:09:49.079 --> 00:09:51.690
resemblance wasn't that convincing.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.270
This sentiment was clearly shared by Fuller,
who placed one hand on either side of Reese's

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:02.130
face and began to softly massage his cheeks,
explaining how disappointed he was that Merrick

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:05.510
didn't actually look all that much like an
elephant.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:10.510
Reese's cheeks started to hurt as the man
massaged him, almost as if he was stretching

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:13.750
and pulling at his face, finding loose skin.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.839
The boy asked Fuller what he was doing.

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:21.380
The circus master just smiled at him and explained
that it was an old technique handed down in

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.720
his family, a gift he called it.

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.620
He started touching Reese's nose, gently pulling
at it with more and more pressure.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:34.380
Much to Reese's surprise, he felt his nose
elongating, growing longer and longer in the

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.380
man's hand.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:39.660
He tried to twitch his face and was startled
to find that his new nose twitched along with

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:40.660
it.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:45.649
The longer it grew, the more painful it felt,
the skin and flesh from his head being ripped

00:10:45.649 --> 00:10:47.890
and pulled into a new shape.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:52.020
At several points, Reese felt ready to pass
out from the agony, but the circus master

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:57.279
kept talking to him, kept him awake through
the whole ordeal as he molded the boy's face

00:10:57.279 --> 00:11:00.550
like clay into the shape of an elephant.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:05.029
Lying on the bench, panting, Reese could see
his trunk from between his two eyes.

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:07.600
It was a nightmare, that was all it was.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.410
But then Fuller reached under the workbench
and lifted out a small hand drill.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:17.690
One by one, he punched holes into the top
of Reese's trunk, installing little valves

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.180
like a trumpet as he went.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:24.910
Standing back to admire his handiwork, he
told Reese that he would be his new favorite

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:25.910
attraction.

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:31.220
‘My boy, I have made you into a star!’

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:32.220
*

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.500
The tusks did not come straight away.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.810
Over the course of several months, they slowly
and painfully emerged from his deformed jaw.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.440
His elephantine legs also were not part of
his initial transformation but instead came

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:48.730
as a result of health issues he developed
while being trapped in a cage for several

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:50.170
decades.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:54.500
Crowds of people would come and go, flocking
to see the Elephant Man.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:59.910
Having spent the first part of his life constantly
hungry, Reese was now being overfed every

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:00.910
day.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:05.450
Hands would come to his cage and force him
to eat rich, fatty foods, denying him any

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.630
possibility of exercise.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:11.779
The broad shoulders and strong legs that he
had developed while working at the circus

00:12:11.779 --> 00:12:15.010
melted away under layers and layers of fat.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:20.360
His skin went gray and papery, folding and
wrinkling in on itself, either from Fuller's

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:23.790
magic or just from a lack of sunlight.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:30.959
Whatever it was, with every passing day, Reese
Freeman steadily became more and more hideous.

00:12:30.959 --> 00:12:33.860
SCP-4409 was stuck in the circus for much
of his life.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:38.610
Eventually, Herman Fuller moved on and is
currently being hunted by the SCP Foundation.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:44.589
When he did, SCP-4409 was able to bargain
for its freedom from the circus.

00:12:44.589 --> 00:12:50.180
Wandering from city to city, never sticking
around for very long, SCP-4409 begged for

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:52.370
change at the side of the road.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:57.160
Some people would feel sympathy, while most
would feel horror at its disfigurement.

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:03.300
From its years trapped in a cage, SCP-4409
has developed several health issues, including

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.899
gout, severe obesity, and metabolic syndrome.

00:13:05.899 --> 00:13:11.410
While in Foundation custody, it is fed a special
diet to counteract these conditions.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:15.930
As of yet, the Foundation has no way of reversing
what was done to Reese Freeman in the tent

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.839
in the 1800s.

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:22.570
As Reese poses no threat to the Foundation,
he is allowed to live with relative freedom,

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:26.180
or at least as much freedom as a contained
SCP can earn.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:31.510
His health condition severely limits his mobility
around Site-66, a low-security residential

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.120
containment area.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:39.240
Unable to walk even moderate distances, SCP-4409
heavily relies on the use of a cane and a

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:44.060
heavy-duty walker capable of supporting up
to 140 kilograms.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:49.279
Researchers listen intently at his door, waiting
to hear if he's ever going to play a sad song

00:13:49.279 --> 00:13:54.990
on his trumpet, but no sound ever emerges,
except for his heavy breathing and sputtering

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:55.990
coughs.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:01.050
It appears that having spent decades locked
in a small cage, a sedentary life is all he

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.050
knows now.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:08.449
With every opportunity to exercise, read books,
and take up new hobbies, SCP-4409 appears

00:14:08.449 --> 00:14:13.250
unable to engage with anything other than
sitting and staring at the same spot on the

00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:14.250
wall every day.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:19.680
If an animal has spent its whole life living
in a zoo and finds, in its old age, that one

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:25.490
of the walls has fallen down, what use has
it in taking a step outside?

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:31.590
Now check out SCP-082 - Fernand the Cannibal
and SCP-5031 - Yet Another Murder Monster!

